<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicholas of Amsdorf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1483–1565</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nikolaus von Amsdorf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicholas of Amsdorf was a German Lutheran theologian and a close colleague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. In 1542 the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> installed him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> against the chapter's Catholic choice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-von-pflug">Julius von Pflug</a>, and Luther consecrated him, but he was driven out after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> in 1547.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-amsdorf.jpg" alt="Nicholas of Amsdorf" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Gottland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Nikolaus_von_Amsdorff_Bishop_of_Naumburg_by_Peter_Gottlandt_1558.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
